Your computer says it cannot save another video. The folder
you're working in contains only a few files, so where did the
space go? Maybe that folder sits on a different disk from the
one you were checking. Maybe the destination is full even
though your home directory has room. We will ask two different
questions: how much room does the destination's filesystem
report, and how much space do the reachable files account for?

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify the filesystem serving a destination by starting with its path.
\item Compare filesystem totals, directory usage, and logical byte counts.
\item Recognize when cleanup is premature or when ordinary counts omit something.
\end{objectives}

\section{Start where the save failed}
\label{sec:34-df}
\index{df@\texttt{df}}
\index{filesystem}
\index{mount point}
The directory tree looks continuous, but it can contain several
filesystems. A filesystem keeps track of names, data, metadata,
and available space. A \FirstUse{mount point} is a directory
where another filesystem's tree becomes accessible. An attached
drive or a network share may each have its own mount point.
An operating system may also split ordinary-looking locations
across several filesystems without showing a drive letter at
every boundary.
For example, the branches in Figure~\ref{fig:34-mount}
share a pathname tree without sharing a storage total.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
df -Pk .
df -Pk "$HOME"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|df| reports a filesystem's accounting. \cmd|-P| requests
its portable layout, and \cmd|-k| requests 1024-byte units.
The operand \cmd|.| is the working directory; \cmd|"$HOME"|
asks about the home directory instead. They may lead to the same
filesystem. If a destination does not exist yet, ask about its
nearest existing parent directory. If a removable drive was
supposed to be mounted there, check the mount before retrying:
otherwise a save directed at an ordinary directory can land on
the wrong filesystem.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-34/mount.tex}
\caption{The pathname crosses a mount point without changing
the syntax used to address the destination.}
\label{fig:34-mount}
\VisualDescription{The root tree leads to a Projects directory
on the main filesystem and Media on another filesystem. Media
is drawn inside a dashed mount boundary.}
\end{figure}

Here is a rearranged short example of the fields, not output
from your system:

\begin{terminaloutput}
Filesystem   1024-blocks   Used Available Capacity Mounted on
/dev/example       12000   8000      3500      70% /media/demo
\end{terminaloutput}

The exact labels and source name vary. The \texttt{Filesystem}
column might report a device, remote source, or a named virtual
filesystem. The mount location is in the far-right column.
``Available'' is generally more relevant for a save than peak
capacity. Do not insist that total minus used must equal available.
Some filesystems reserve space, account for areas differently, or
make allocations conditional. In this illustration capacity is
the rounded ratio of used space to the space used or available
to ordinary users; reserved space is not part of that denominator.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1in}YY}
\toprule
Question & Useful observation & Blind spot\\
\midrule
Can I save here? & \texttt{df -Pk} for the destination's parent. &
Quota, file size limits, permissions, and application scratch needs.\\
What takes space? & \texttt{du -sk} on a directory you can search. &
Objects outside it or not available through its names.\\
How many bytes can I read? & \texttt{wc -c} on a single ordinary file. &
Allocation overhead and shared or sparse storage.\\
Can I make new names? & Local inode or quota reporting where supported. &
``Bytes free'' does not prove that another object can be created.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

An inode is a filesystem object record on filesystems that use
that terminology. A filesystem can exhaust its supply of records
even when it has room for some additional bytes. Many Linux,
macOS, and BSD versions of \cmd|df| offer \cmd|-i| views; read
the local \ManPage{df}{1} before relying on a field or writing a
script. If the error mentions a quota, the filesystem as a whole
may have space while your account or project has reached a limit.
On a shared machine, ask the administrator; clearing other
people's files is neither a test nor a fix.

\section{Measure what you can name}
\label{sec:34-du}
\index{du@\texttt{du}}
Use \cmd|sh| for the examples. Run the setup line separately
from a writable directory, and proceed only if it prints the
fresh practice location. \cmd|mktemp -d| creates a directory
from the supplied template, replacing the Xs. It is widely
available on our target families but is not POSIX.

\begin{terminal}
room=$(mktemp -d ./utp-space.XXXXXX) && cd "$room" && pwd
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
mkdir ./notes
printf 'a short list\n' > ./notes/list.txt
wc -c < ./notes/list.txt
du -k ./notes/list.txt
\end{terminal}

The byte count is 13, including the ending newline. \cmd|du|
asks how much storage is allocated to named items. Its
\cmd|-k| selects the same unit size as \cmd|df -k|,
not the same accounting question. A tiny text file often
uses at least an allocation block, but allocation strategy is
up to the filesystem. The number could vary with inline data,
block sizes, compression, or how the system reports shared
storage. Don't teach your future self to expect ``4'' just
because a machine reports it today.

\begin{terminal}
du -sk ./notes
du -sk .
df -Pk .
\end{terminal}

\cmd|du -s| asks for a summary rather than a line for every
subdirectory. The operand controls where it starts. The
outer summary can include directories and their own allocation.
The \cmd|df| view doesn't belong to this one directory; it
reports the whole filesystem serving it. Running it on
\cmd|./notes| should show the same filesystem as \cmd|.|
unless something independent of the exercise changes the
mount layout. The byte counts or available space of a live
system may change while you are looking.
Before comparing two counts, trace the scopes in
Figure~\ref{fig:34-scope}: a small subtree can be only a
small part of a much larger allocation pool.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-34/scope.tex}
\caption{\texttt{du} follows a branch of names; \texttt{df}
asks the filesystem for a total.}
\label{fig:34-scope}
\VisualDescription{Inside a box labeled filesystem total there
are notes and other branches. A smaller dashed boundary around
notes shows the scope of a du summary started there.}
\end{figure}

\begin{terminal}
printf 'hidden item\n' > ./.note
du -sk .
du -sk ./notes ./.note
\end{terminal}

The first summary starts at the entire directory, including
hidden names it can reach; it isn't based on a shell glob.
The second gives separate summaries for the two explicit
operands and does not account for the outer directory itself.
If you report only files selected by \cmd|*| you can miss
hidden entries: the shell filters the names before \cmd|du|
arrives. A disk-use scanner can also be slowed by a deep tree
or a network share under it. Use a small specific path rather
than scanning the whole system. Where documented, \cmd|du -x|
stays on the device of the starting operand rather than
descending to other mounted filesystems.

\begin{terminal}
ls -l ./notes/list.txt
du -k ./notes/list.txt
\end{terminal}

The size in the long listing is the file's logical byte length.
For a sparse file that length can exceed the stored data by a
wide margin: the filesystem can represent ranges of zeros
without allocating the corresponding data blocks. A backup
tool that materializes those zeros during a copy could use
much more space than the source appeared to use. This is
one reason to check backups as actual, readable archives
rather than only comparing the size numbers.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.25in}YY}
\toprule
Situation & What to inspect & What not to infer\\
\midrule
Directory summary small & Check mount, inaccessible subtrees,
and whether the rest of the filesystem is outside your scope. &
The disk report is necessarily wrong.\\
Large byte size, low usage & Consider sparse allocation,
compression, or sharing. & Every logical byte occupies a unique byte
of storage.\\
Space didn't return after removal & Consider open files,
other links, snapshots, and unrelated new writes. &
The only remedy is to remove more.\\
Error with space available & Check quota, destination permissions,
object limits, and scratch location. & Free bytes settle every
``cannot save'' problem.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{The invisible occupants}
\label{sec:34-unlinked}
\index{open file!after unlink}
\index{snapshot}
Removing a name is an operation on a directory, as
Chapter~\ref{ch:33} explains. If the last hard link to an
ordinary file is removed while a process still has the file
open, its data remains accessible through that open handle.
That data takes up storage until the last applicable reference
closes. A walk that starts from names can no longer see the
file, but the filesystem still accounts for it. Think of
deleting a catalog entry while a borrower still holds the book.
The analogy stops there: the name won't spring back when the
borrower closes the file.
Figure~\ref{fig:34-unlinked} separates the open handle
from the name a directory walk could have reached.

There is no need to make a drive run out of space to see
this difference. The reusable \texttt{space-check.sh}
listing uses a disposable tiny fixture, not a large test
file: its measurement of allocation is useful even when
the output rounds differently. To see a related naming
effect without disturbing open applications, think
back to hard links in Chapter~\ref{ch:8}. A file can have
two names, so one removed name may leave the object fully
accessible through the other. When summing disk use,
don't assume two names belonging to one filesystem object
therefore represent two independently allocated copies.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-34/unlinked.tex}
\caption{Removing the last name does not close an already open file.}
\label{fig:34-unlinked}
\VisualDescription{A crossed-out arrow from the directory name
and an intact arrow from an open process handle both point
toward the file data.}
\end{figure}

On some systems an optional tool such as \ManPage{lsof}{8}
or a local system tool can reveal such open objects, subject to
visibility and permissions. Don't kill a process or restart a
service to free space unless you are responsible for it and
understand the consequences. If it is your app, try the app's
normal close procedure and check again. If the deleted file
contained your only copy, don't rely on open handles as a
recovery technique; keep the app open while seeking appropriate
help and use a tested backup if you have one. Chapter~\ref{ch:41}
shows how to verify a restore before it is an emergency.

\section{macOS: volumes, containers, and snapshots}
\label{sec:34-apfs}
\index{APFS}
On macOS, an APFS container can supply space to several
volumes. They are not several independent disks each with the
full amount of space shown in its free-space column. Two
volumes in one container can both report that pool of space;
adding those readings counts capacity twice. A volume can also
have a reserve or quota. A \FirstUse{snapshot} preserves the
state of a volume at a point in time, so removing the visible
name of an old version may not release all of the storage
used by that version. Snapshots do not behave like ordinary
directories under the location you scan with \cmd|du|.

These are macOS-only, read-only commands. Run just the
first pair if you are comparing such views for your own
Mac. They are not instructions to erase, repair, or mount
a disk. The local \ManPage{diskutil}{8} and
\ManPage{tmutil}{8} pages describe the supported subcommands.

\begin{terminal}
df -Pk "$HOME"
diskutil info /
\end{terminal}

\cmd|diskutil info| shows information about the disk or
volume identified here by the root mount path. Look for
identifiers and filesystem information; field names vary
with the system and storage. Root and your home directory
are not necessarily on the same volume, especially on modern
macOS systems that distinguish System and Data. For a
removable drive, first confirm its exact path in Finder or
Disk Utility; ask \cmd|df -Pk| about that path rather than
assuming the drive shares the boot volume's available space.

\begin{terminal}
diskutil apfs list
\end{terminal}

Here \cmd|apfs| chooses a family of disk information and
management commands; use only its \cmd|list| subcommand for
this check. It shows the APFS containers and associated
volumes that the tool can see. Try matching a device
identifier from \cmd|df| or \cmd|diskutil info| with the
identifiers it lists, noting that a mounted snapshot can
show its own identifier. Don't rely on a volume name such
as ``Data'' alone: names are not required to be unique.
Keep serial numbers, identifiers, and personal volume
names out of a public report unless there is a specific
reason to share a redacted version.

\begin{terminal}
tmutil listlocalsnapshots /
\end{terminal}

\cmd|tmutil| is the Time Machine utility. The subcommand
shown lists local Time Machine snapshots for the specified
mount point where supported; it does not remove them and is
not a complete inventory of every snapshot on every volume.
The graphical Disk Utility can also show APFS snapshots
for a selected volume via its View menu. A date in a name
does not tell you how much space would be released or
whether the snapshot is the recovery copy you need.
Do not delete snapshots to make two totals match. If the
session needs working space today, first confirm a usable
backup and consider a known destination with room for
both the new file and any temporary data the application
uses. Use a restore drill before relying on a backup.

On FreeBSD or Linux with ZFS, the local dataset and snapshot
tools likewise answer questions a directory walk cannot.
OpenBSD and NetBSD have their own supported filesystems and
tools; use the local storage documentation rather than
pasting APFS or ZFS commands into an unfamiliar system.
GNU and many BSD/macOS tools offer human-readable reports
such as \cmd|df -h|; that presentation is convenient to
read, but rounding and unit conventions make it a poor
interchange format.

\section{Plan a copy that fits}
\label{sec:34-copy}
Put the two reports to work before moving a large collection.
Suppose your original is on your computer and the intended
copy is on a removable drive. Ask \cmd|df -Pk| about the
drive's confirmed mount path and \cmd|du -sk| about the
specific source directory. If \cmd|df| identifies the same
filesystem for both paths when you expected different ones,
stop before copying. You could otherwise fill the drive
you were trying to unload. If a path is ambiguous, locate it
in your file manager or find the correct mount in the
operating system's disk tool. Do not create an alternate
directory merely to silence a missing-path error.

Suppose the expected filesystem has ample room. That still
isn't permission to erase the original after the copy starts.
Logical length, allocation, and transfer behavior can differ,
especially with sparse or cloned files. The copying tool
may need temporary space and the application's format may
change the overall size if you export rather than copy.
Some removable filesystems also limit the size of one file
even when total capacity is fine. Treat the measured space as
planning information and keep the original until you've
verified the destination and checked that important files
actually open. Avoid making a new backup on a nearly full
drive that also contains your only older restore point.

Consider the opposite case. The destination has no room, and
a large directory appears at the top of your usage survey.
That directory could contain source material, backups,
or cache files with very different recovery costs. Its size
does not tell you which it is. Use the application's own
storage management for replaceable data where possible.
Moving older files to a known good independent volume can
be safer than deleting them, but check copy integrity
before removing anything. Moving a directory elsewhere on
the same filesystem usually just changes names and won't
free space. Moving items to a trash folder on the same
volume can likewise leave the storage allocated until
the trash is emptied, at which point easy recovery may be
gone.

If the source directory includes an unreadable part,
the usage estimate can be incomplete. A permission
diagnostic is evidence to keep, not noise to hide. Use the
access rules of Chapter~\ref{ch:33} on your own path and ask
the owner about the rest. You can still report a useful
conclusion: ``I confirmed the target volume, measured the
part of the source I can read, and have not yet estimated
the inaccessible part.'' Uncertainty, stated at the point
it enters the measurement, is better than a confident
but wrong estimate.

Finally, a filesystem report made a week ago cannot
guarantee space for today's copy. On a network share
someone else may be using the same storage pool.
Recheck when you start, and keep enough room to finish
without depending on a particular user to remove files
midway through. If a copy fails partway through, label
that destination as incomplete and retain the originals.
Inspect what was produced before resuming, rewriting, or
removing it. A partial copy's allocation can explain why
the second attempt starts with less space than the first,
but it doesn't tell you what parts of its contents are
usable.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Which number is wrong?}
\Brian{My folders only add up to half of what the disk says is used.}
\Stephen{You have measured the names you counted.}
\Brian{I counted all the names I could see. Isn't that the whole disk?}
\Stephen{There could be other branches, permissions, or data with no visible name.}
\Brian{So nobody promised the two numbers had the same scope.}
\Stephen{Correct. Check the destination's filesystem first, then investigate.}
\Brian{And don't delete another folder just to make the numbers match.}
\Stephen{Exactly. First decide what is safe and useful to remove.}
\DialogueConclusion{Different scopes give different totals.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{exercise}{Locate the destination before measuring it}
\label{ex:34-mount}
Ask which filesystem serves \texttt{notes} and compare the
answer to the filesystem for the current directory. Use the
mount location rather than only comparing available space.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|df -Pk ./notes| and \cmd|df -Pk .|. Both paths
should use the same filesystem and show the same mount
location. Available space can shift during the comparison.
No cleanup is needed for a report.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Count the bytes and allocated units}
\label{ex:34-units}
Create one tiny file with a known number of bytes and measure
its length and disk usage separately. Explain why there is no
required numeric match.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf 'three\n' > ./extra.txt
wc -c < ./extra.txt
du -k ./extra.txt
rm -i ./extra.txt
\end{terminal}
The count is six; the disk usage describes allocation in
1024-byte units. Confirm removal only for the new practice
file.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Make the hidden operand visible}
\label{ex:34-hidden}
Compare the list of operands expanded from a star with the
entries in a listing that includes hidden names. Ask
\cmd|du| for the hidden name explicitly.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' ./*
ls -a
du -k ./.note
\end{terminal}
With the normal \cmd|sh| glob semantics the star omits
\texttt{.note}. \cmd|ls -a| includes hidden entries, and
\cmd|du| can report an explicitly named hidden file. This
exercise does not add a file.
\end{solution}

After checking \cmd|pwd|, remove only the two original
practice files. Confirm only those names at the prompts.
The nonrecursive \cmd|rmdir| checks that no other material is
left in each practice directory before removing it.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./notes/list.txt ./.note
rmdir ./notes
cd .. && rmdir "$room"
unset room
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Ask \texttt{df} about an existing destination path and verify its mount.
\item Ask \texttt{du} about the particular tree you can navigate.
\item Logical length, allocated storage, free space, and account
limits are different things.
\end{takeaways}

